  "title": "Nephrotic Syndrome Cohorts",
  "bodyHtml": "<div class=\"row\"><div class=\"col-md-9\"><h5 class=\"dk-under-header\">The samples currently in the NephKP are an analysis of samples from three cohorts from the principal investigators Friedhelm Hildebrandt, Martin Pollak, and Simone Sanna-Cherchi. All samples were analyzed from raw sequence data. Variant calling and quality control were performed to generate the results visible on NephKP Gene Pages.</h5><p>&nbsp;</p><h2>Summary</h2><p>&nbsp;</p><h4 class=\"dk-blue-bordered\">Cases by phenotype (5,255 cases total)</h4><table class=\"table table-condensed table-responsive table-striped table-bordered\"><thead><tr><th>Population</th><th>Steroid Resistant</th><th>Steroid Sensitive</th><th>Uncategorized</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adult</td><td>1,376</td><td>907</td><td>20</td><td>2,303</td></tr><tr><td>Pediatric</td><td>1,773</td><td>1,083</td><td>58</td><td>2,914</td></tr><tr><td>Unknown</td><td>29</td><td>7</td><td>2</td><td>38</td></tr><tr><td>All samples</td><td>3,178</td><td>1,997</td><td>80</td><td>5,255</td></tr></tbody></table><!--\r\n        <h4 class=\"dk-blue-bordered\">\r\n            Final case counts\r\n        </h4>\r\n        <table class=\"table table-condensed table-responsive table-striped table-bordered\">\r\n            \t\r\n            <thead>\r\n                \t\t\r\n                <tr>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <th>\r\n                        Phenotype\r\n                    </th>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <th>\r\n                        Meta-analysis (all cohorts)\r\n                    </th>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <th>\r\n                        Meta-analysis (Hildebrandt + Sanna-Cherchi)\r\n                    </th>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <th>\r\n                        Meta-analysis (Pollak + Sanna-Cherchi)\r\n                    </th>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <th>\r\n                        Hildebrandt\r\n                    </th>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <th>\r\n                        Pollak\r\n                    </th>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <th>\r\n                        Sanna-Cherchi\r\n                    </th>\r\n                    \t\t\r\n                </tr>\r\n                \t\r\n            </thead>\r\n            \t\r\n            <tbody>\r\n                \t\t\r\n                <tr>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        All Nephrotic Syndrome cases\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        874\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        408\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        105\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        361\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\r\n                </tr>\r\n                \t\t\r\n                <tr>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        Nephrotic Syndrome, uncategorized*\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        239\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        34\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        105\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        100\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\r\n                </tr>\r\n                \t\t\r\n                <tr>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        Nephrotic Syndrome, steroid resistant*\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        380\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        318\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        62\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\r\n                </tr>\r\n                \t\t\r\n                <tr>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        Nephrotic Syndrome, steroid sensitive*\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        228\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        26\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        202\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\r\n                </tr>\r\n                \t\t\r\n                <tr>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        Focal Segmental Glomerulosclerosis (FSGS)\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        1,316\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        333\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        983\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\r\n                </tr>\r\n                \t\t\r\n                <tr>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        Minimal Change Disease (MCD)\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        297\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\r\n                </tr>\r\n                \t\t\r\n                <tr>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        Healthy\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        16\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        NA\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\r\n                </tr>\r\n                \t\r\n            </tbody>\r\n        </table>\r\n        <p>\r\n            *Some samples in this category are also included in the FSGS samples.\r\n        </p>\r\n        --><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Find the details of the analysis below:</p><ul><li><a href=\"#calling\">Variant calling</a></li><li><a href=\"#QC\">Quality control</a></li></ul><h4 class=\"dk-blue-bordered\">Variant calling</h4><!--\r\n        <p>\r\n            Variant calling was performed on the Hildebrandt cohort using the Variant Quality Score Recalibration (<a href=\"https://gatk.broadinstitute.org/hc/en-us/articles/360035531612-Variant-Quality-Score-Recalibration-VQSR-\" target=\"_blank\">VQSR</a>) pipeline of the Genome Analysis Toolkit (<a href=\"https://gatk.broadinstitute.org/hc/en-us\" target=\"_blank\">GATK</a>) version 4.1.7.0. The GVCF files of all samples were merged and joint genotyping was performed. A recalibration table of the samples was created; based on the table, a score cutoff was applied to filter the variants.\r\n        </p>\r\n        <p>\r\n            Variant calling was performed on the Pollak cohort using the <a href=\"https://terra.bio/\" target=\"_blank\">Terra</a> platform. Sample IDs were derived from file names, duplicates were removed, and GATK workflows were used to convert both the CRAM and FASTQ files to unmapped BAM. Next, the GATK Best Practices pipeline was run. This pipeline includes preprocessing, haplotype calling, and joint genotyping.\r\n        </p>\r\n        <p>\r\n            Variant calling on the Sanna-Cherchi cohort was performed as follows:\r\n        </p>\r\n        --><p>In brief, reads were aligned to human reference GRCh37 using DRAGEN (1), duplicates were marked with Picard and variants were called according to the Genome Analysis Toolkit (GATK) Best Practices recommendations v3.6 (2,3). As described in detail in Ren <em>et al.</em> 2021 (4), the centralized ATAV database was used to store sample-level variant calls and various associated quality metrics, as well as all sites’ coverage data for inferring reference alleles at non-call sites. To reduce the influence of coverage differences caused by different capture kits or sequencing depth in general, we used the site-based pruning approach described in Petrovski <em>et al.</em> 2017 (5). As demonstrated before, this approach enables the analysis of exomes and genomes, samples sequenced with different exome capture kits, and samples sequenced on different platforms while reducing the risk of detecting signal that is only caused by the fact that a position is not well covered (5–10). We calculated the absolute difference in percentages of cases compared to controls with at least 10x coverage and only kept sites with a difference of less or equal 7%. In an effort to include only high-quality variants, we applied the basic variant quality control filters listed in Povysil et al. 2020 (10).</p><p>References for variant calling:</p><p>1. Miller, N. A. et al. A 26-hour system of highly sensitive whole genome sequencing for emergency management of genetic diseases. Genome Med. 7, 100 (2015).<br>2. McKenna, A. et al. The Genome Analysis Toolkit: a MapReduce framework for analyzing next-generation DNA sequencing data. Genome Res. 20, 1297–303 (2010).<br>3. Van der Auwera, G. A. et al. From FastQ Data to High-Confidence Variant Calls: The Genome Analysis Toolkit Best Practices Pipeline. Curr. Protoc. Bioinforma. 43, 11.10.1-11.10.33 (2013).<br>4. Ren, Z. et al. ATAV: a comprehensive platform for population-scale genomic analyses. BMC Bioinformatics 22, 149 (2021).<br>5. Petrovski, S. et al. An Exome Sequencing Study to Assess the Role of Rare Genetic Variation in Pulmonary Fibrosis. Am. J. Respir. Crit. Care Med. 196, 82–93 (2017).<br>6. Cameron-Christie, S. et al. Exome-Based Rare-Variant Analyses in CKD. J. Am. Soc. Nephrol. JASN 30, 1109–1122 (2019).<br>7. Povysil, G. et al. Rare-variant collapsing analyses for complex traits: guidelines and applications. Nat. Rev. Genet. 1–13 (2019).<br>8. Allen, A. S. et al. Ultra-rare genetic variation in common epilepsies: a case-control sequencing study. Lancet Neurol. 16, 135–143 (2017).<br>9. Gelfman, S. et al. A new approach for rare variation collapsing on functional protein domains implicates specific genic regions in ALS. Genome Res. 29, 809–818 (2019).<br>10. Povysil, G. et al. Assessing the Role of Rare Genetic Variation in Patients with Heart Failure. JAMA Cardiol. 6, 379–386 (2020).</p><p>&nbsp;</p><h4 class=\"dk-blue-bordered\">Quality control</h4><p>Quality control was performed to detect samples and variants that did not fit our standards for inclusion in analysis. The highest quality variants were used in a battery of tests to assess the quality of each sample. Duplicate pairs, samples exhibiting excessive sharing of identity by descent, samples whose genotypic sex did not match their clinical sex, and outliers detected among several sample-by-variant statistics were removed from further analysis. Additionally, genotypic ancestry was inferred with respect to a modern reference panel, allowing for variant filtering and association analyses to be performed within populations as needed.</p><p>Prior to rare variant analysis, we inferred ancestry in relation to a modern reference panel representing the major human populations. While our particular sample QC process does not directly depend on this information, it is useful to downstream analysis when stratifying the calculation of certain variant statistics that are sensitive to population substructure (<em>e.g.</em>, Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium). Initially, the samples were merged with the entire set of 2,504 1000 Genomes Phase 3 Version 5 samples; our method restricts this merging to a set of 5,835 known ancestry informative SNPs. After merging, principal components (PCs) were computed using FlashPCA2. Using the principal components of ancestry as features, we performed Gaussian mixture modelling on the samples and identified clusters of samples aligning with reference population groups: AFR, AMR, EUR, EAS, and SAS. Samples that did not seem to fit into a well-defined major population group were removed from further analysis.</p><p>Sample pair kinship coefficients were determined using KING relationship inference software, which offers a robust algorithm for relationship inference under population stratification. Variants exhibiting non-A/C/G/T alleles, low call rate, or low minor allele frequency and variants with positions in known high LD regions or on non-autosomal chromosomes were removed prior to kinship calculations. Duplicate sample pairs were removed from downstream analysis. Any single individual exhibiting kinship values indicating a 2nd degree relative or higher relationship with 10 or more others was flagged for removal as well. Where clinical data were available, each batch of data was checked for genotype / clinical data agreement for sex and any mismatching samples were removed from further analysis.</p><p>Each sample was evaluated for inclusion based on 10 sample-by-variant metrics:</p><ul><li><strong>call_rate</strong>: Fraction of variants with called genotypes</li><li><strong>het</strong>: Inbreeding coefficient</li><li><strong>het_high</strong>: Inbreeding coefficient for variants with MAF ≥ 0.03</li><li><strong>het_low</strong>: Inbreeding coefficient for variants with MAF &lt; 0.03</li><li><strong>n_het</strong>: Number of heterozygous variants</li><li><strong>n_hom_var</strong>: Number of homozygous alternate variants</li><li><strong>n_non_ref</strong>: n_het + n_hom_var</li><li><strong>n_called</strong>: n_hom_ref + n_het + n_hom_var</li><li><strong>r_ti_tv</strong>: Transition/transversion ratio</li><li><strong>r_het_hom_var</strong>: heterozygous / homozygous alternate allele ratio</li></ul><p>Note that for the metrics n_called and call_rate, only samples below the mean are filtered. Due to possible population substructure, the sample metrics exhibit some multi-modality in their distributions. To evaluate more normally distributed data, we calculated principal component adjusted residuals of the metrics using the top 10 principal components (PCARM's). For outlier detection, we clustered the samples into Gaussian distributed subsets with respect to each PCARM using Gaussian mixture modelling. During this process, samples that did not fit into any Gaussian distributed set of samples were identified and removed. In addition to outliers along individual sample metrics, we also identified samples that exhibited deviation from the norm across multiple metrics. In order to identify samples of this nature, we calculated principal components explaining 95% of the variation in 8 of the 10 PCARMs combined. The adjusted residuals for metrics 'call_rate' and 'n_called' are characterized by long tails that lead to the maximum value, which is not consistent with the other metrics. In order to avoid excessive removal of samples with lower, yet still completely acceptable, call rates, these metrics are left out of principal component calculation. All samples were clustered into Gaussian distributed subsets along the principal components of the PCARM's. This effectively removes any samples that are far enough outside the distribution on more than one PCARM, but not necessarily an outlier on any of the individual metrics alone.</p><p>In order to remove problematic variants, filters were applied for call-rate and heterozygosity:</p><ul><li>call_rate ≥ 0.3</li><li>het &lt; 1</li></ul><h5>Number of relationships found by type in the unified sample set and its three constituent cohorts</h5><table class=\"table table-condensed table-responsive table-striped table-bordered\"><thead><tr><th>Relationship type</th><th>NephKP Joint Analysis</th><th>Hildebrandt cohort</th><th>Pollak cohort</th><th>Sanna-Cherchi cohort</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>All</td><td>1</td><td>186</td><td>499</td><td>1,806</td></tr><tr><td>Dup/MZ</td><td>0</td><td>83</td><td>309</td><td>1,804</td></tr><tr><td>Parent/Offspring</td><td>0</td><td>53</td><td>95</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Full sibling</td><td>0</td><td>26</td><td>50</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>2nd degree</td><td>1</td><td>18</td><td>38</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>3rd degree</td><td>0</td><td>5</td><td>5</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table><h5><a href=\"https://hugeampkpncms.org/sites/default/files/Documents/NephKP.qc_report.pdf\" target=\"_blank\">Download the detailed quality control report</a></h5><p>&nbsp;</p><!--\r\n        <h4 class=\"dk-blue-bordered\">\r\n            <a name=\"MA\"></a>Meta-analysis\r\n        </h4>\r\n        <p>\r\n            After quality control, the 3 datasets were checked for cross-cohort relationships. The resulting disjoint sets were then used for meta-analysis. The process for generating variant counts initially followed our typical meta-analysis pipeline. Due to some datasets lacking certain phenotypes, there are 3 possible meta-analyses we performed to maximize sample inclusion for each phenotype. For each possible meta analysis, common variants were extracted from each cohort's filtered QC dataset (<em>i.e</em>., the dataset used to calculate kinship during QC process) and then the datasets were merged into a single dataset. King was run on the resulting dataset to find cross cohort relationships of degree of no more than 2.\r\n        </p>\r\n        <p>\r\n            &nbsp;Table 1. Common variant count by meta-analysis.&nbsp;\r\n        </p>\r\n        <table class=\"table table-condensed table-responsive table-striped table-bordered\">\r\n            \t\r\n            <thead>\r\n                \t\t\r\n                <tr>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <th>\r\n                        Meta-analysis\r\n                    </th>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <th>\r\n                        Common variant count\r\n                    </th>\r\n                    \t\t\r\n                </tr>\r\n                \t\r\n            </thead>\r\n            \t\r\n            <tbody>\r\n                \t\t\r\n                <tr>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        All 3 cohorts\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        31,938\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\r\n                </tr>\r\n                \t\t\r\n                <tr>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        Hildebrandt + Sanna-Cherchi\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        35,005\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\r\n                </tr>\r\n                \t\t\r\n                <tr>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        Pollak&nbsp;+ Sanna-Cherchi\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        38,216\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\r\n                </tr>\r\n                \t\r\n            </tbody>\r\n        </table>\r\n        <p>\r\n            &nbsp;\r\n        </p>\r\n        <p>\r\n            Table 2. Cross-cohort related pairs by meta-analysis.\r\n        </p>\r\n        <table class=\"table table-condensed table-responsive table-striped table-bordered\">\r\n            \t\r\n            <thead>\r\n                \t\t\r\n                <tr>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <th>\r\n                        Meta-analysis\r\n                    </th>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <th>\r\n                        Hildebrandt-Hildebrandt\r\n                    </th>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <th>\r\n                        Pollak-Pollak\r\n                    </th>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <th>\r\n                        Sanna-Cherchi-Sanna-Cherchi\r\n                    </th>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <th>\r\n                        Hildebrandt-Pollak\r\n                    </th>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <th>\r\n                        Pollak-Sanna-Cherchi\r\n                    </th>\r\n                    \t\t\r\n                </tr>\r\n                \t\r\n            </thead>\r\n            \t\r\n            <tbody>\r\n                \t\t\r\n                <tr>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        All 3 cohorts\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        258\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\