                       Pollak-Sanna-Cherchi\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        0\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        0\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        16\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        3\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <td>\r\n                        4\r\n                    </td>\r\n                    \t\t\r\n                </tr>\r\n                \t\r\n            </tbody>\r\n        </table>\r\n        <p>\r\n            For each phenotype, samples were included in a hierarchical way. First, all available Hildebrandt cohort samples were included, then unrelated Pollak cohort samples, and finally, any unrelated Sanna-Cherchi cohort samples. This resulted in a disjoint set of samples from each cohort. For each phenotype and each disjoint sample set, we calculated principal components of ancestry and performed up to 10 iterations of outlier removal. In each iteration, adjusted residuals were calculated from the top 10 principal components. Outlier samples were defined as more than 6 standard deviations from the mean on any of the nominally associated PCs and were subsequently removed. Iteration ended if no outliers were found or 10 iterations was reached.\r\n        </p>\r\n        <p>\r\n            Table 6. Final sample inclusion by phenotype.\r\n        </p>\r\n        <table class=\"table table-condensed table-responsive table-striped table-bordered\">\r\n            \t\r\n            <thead>\r\n                \t\t\r\n                <tr>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n                    <th>\r\n                        Phenotype\r\n                    </th>\r\n                    \t\t\t\r\n